{
  "title": "Low‑Poly Rock Generator",
  "kind": "blender",
  "summary": "A Blender 5 script that creates a ground plane and scatters a configurable number of low‑poly rocks over it. Each rock is built from an icosphere, randomly displaced via BMesh, flat‑shaded, and given a grey stone material driven by a Noise Texture. The placement uses ray‑casting to drop rocks onto the ground, and all randomness is seeded for reproducibility.",
  "steps": [
    {
      "id": "s1",
      "title": "Initialize script and set seed",
      "kind": "blender",
      "instructions": "Import `bpy`, `bmesh`, `random`, and `mathutils`. Set a deterministic random seed (default 42) using `random.seed(seed)` and `mathutils.noise.seed_set(seed)`. Define a constant `ROCK_COUNT` (default 8) and expose it as a script parameter. No objects should be created in this step.",
      "depends_on": []
    },
    {
      "id": "s2",
      "title": "Define rock‑generation function",
      "kind": "blender",
      "instructions": "Using the BMesh API, write a function `create_rock(name, location, radius=0.5, displacement=0.2)` that:\n1. Adds an icosphere mesh (subdivisions=1) at the origin.\n2. Randomly displaces each vertex along its normal by a value in `[-displacement, displacement]` using the seeded `random`.\n3. Recalculates normals and applies flat shading (`mesh.use_auto_smooth = False`).\n4. Moves the object to `location`.\n5. Returns the created object. The function must not assign any material – that is handled later.\nMake sure the function is pure and can be called multiple times without side effects.",
      "depends_on": ["s1"]
    },
    {
      "id": "s3",
      "title": "Create grey stone material with Noise Texture",
      "kind": "blender",
      "instructions": "Create a new material named `RockMaterial` using the Principled BSDF. Add a Noise Texture node (Scale=5, Detail=2) and connect its Color output to the Base Color of the Principled BSDF. Set the Principled BSDF Roughness to 0.6 and Metallic to 0.0. Ensure the material uses the `ShaderNodeBsdfPrincipled` and is set to `use_nodes = True`. The material should be returned or stored in a variable `rock_mat` for later assignment.",
      "depends_on": ["s1"]
    },
    {
      "id": "s4",
      "title": "Create ground plane",
      "kind": "blender",
      "instructions": "Add a large plane (size 20) at the origin, name it `Ground`. Apply a simple grey material (or reuse `rock_mat` if desired) and ensure the plane has `collision` enabled for ray‑casting. The plane must be a mesh object and should be returned or stored as `ground_obj`.",
      "depends_on": ["s1"]
    },
    {
      "id": "s5",
      "title": "Scatter rocks, ray‑cast drop, and assign material",
      "kind": "blender",
      "instructions": "Using the `ROCK_COUNT` constant, loop to create rocks:\n1. For each iteration, generate a random X/Y location within a radius of 8 units around the origin (use `random.uniform(-8, 8)`). Z is initially set high (e.g., 5).\n2. Call `create_rock` from step s2 with a unique name (e.g., `Rock_01`).\n3. Perform a ray‑cast from the rock’s location downwards (`Vector((0,0,-1))`) onto `ground_obj` using `bpy.context.scene.ray_cast`. Move the rock to the hit location so it sits on the plane.\n4. Assign `rock_mat` (from step s3) to the rock’s mesh material slot.\n5. After placement, set the object’s shading to flat (`obj.data.use_auto_smooth = False`).\nFinally, group all rocks into a collection named `Rocks_Collection` and parent the collection to the scene root.\nThe script should leave the scene ready for rendering.",
      "depends_on": ["s2", "s3", "s4"]
    }
  ]
}

{
  "title": "Home Teleport Mod",
  "kind": "code",
  "summary": "A Fabric mod for Minecraft 1.20.2 that adds /sethome and /home commands. /sethome records a player's position, /home teleports after a 3‑second warm‑up that aborts if the player moves or takes damage. Home data is persisted in a JSON file inside the world folder.",
  "steps": [
    {
      "id": "s1",
      "title": "Create Fabric project skeleton",
      "kind": "code",
      "instructions": "Generate a new Fabric mod project using the official template. Include:\n- `build.gradle` with the correct Minecraft version (1.20.2) and Fabric loader version.\n- `fabric.mod.json` with a unique mod id (e.g., \"home_teleport_mod\"), name, description, and entrypoint class `com.example.hometp.HomeTeleportMod`.\n- Main class `HomeTeleportMod` that implements `ModInitializer` and registers the mod's event listeners in `onInitialize`.\nProvide the full file contents for `build.gradle`, `fabric.mod.json`, and `HomeTeleportMod.java`.\nNo external dependencies beyond Fabric API.",
      "depends_on": []
    },
    {
      "id": "s2",
      "title": "Implement persistent home storage",
      "kind": "code",
      "instructions": "Create class `HomeStorage` in package `com.example.hometp`. It must:\n- Load a JSON file named `homes.json` from `world.getSavePath(WorldSavePath.ROOT).resolve(\"homes.json\")` when the world loads.\n- Store a map of player UUID → `HomePos` (a simple POJO with x, y, z, yaw, pitch).\n- Provide methods `setHome(ServerPlayerEntity player, Vec3d pos, float yaw, float pitch)`, `Optional<HomePos> getHome(UUID uuid)`, `save()` and `load()`.\n- Use Gson (already bundled with Minecraft) for serialization.\n- Ensure thread‑safety (synchronize on the map) and call `save()` after each `setHome`.\nReturn the complete Java source for `HomeStorage.java` and the `HomePos` record.\n",
      "depends_on": ["s1"]
    },
    {
      "id": "s3",
      "title": "Register /sethome and /home commands",
      "kind": "code",
      "instructions": "In the mod initializer (`HomeTeleportMod.onInitialize`), register two commands using Fabric's CommandRegistrationCallback:\n- `/sethome` – stores the invoking player's current position via `HomeStorage.setHome`. Respond with a green chat message confirming the home was set.\n- `/home` – checks if the player has a saved home. If not, send a red error message. If yes, start a 3‑second warm‑up by calling `WarmupManager.startWarmup(player, homePos)`.\nProvide the command registration code snippet and any helper methods needed.\n",
      "depends_on": ["s2"]
    },
    {
      "id": "s4",
      "title": "Create warm‑up manager with cancellation",
      "kind": "code",
      "instructions": "Add class `WarmupManager` in package `com.example.hometp`. It must:\n- Keep a `Map<UUID, WarmupTask>` of active warm‑ups.\n- `startWarmup(ServerPlayerEntity player, HomePos target)` schedules a `ServerTask` (use `MinecraftServer.getTickScheduler()` or `ServerTickEvents.END_SERVER_TICK`) that counts down 60 ticks (3 seconds).\n- After the countdown, teleport the player with `player.teleport(target.x, target.y, target.z, target.yaw, target.pitch)` and send a success message.\n- Provide `cancelWarmup(UUID uuid, String reason)` to abort and notify the player.\n- Expose `isWarmingUp(UUID uuid)` for the event listeners.\nReturn the full Java source for `WarmupManager.java` and any inner classes.\n",
      "depends_on": ["s2"]
    },
    {
      "id": "s5",
      "title": "Hook movement and damage events to cancel warm‑up",
      "kind": "code",
      "instructions": "In `HomeTeleportMod.onInitialize`, register the following Fabric events:\n- `ServerPlayConnectionEvents.JOIN` to clear any stale warm‑up entries.\n- `PlayerMoveEvents.AFTER` (or `ServerPlayerEvents.COPY_FROM`) to detect any position change while a warm‑up is active; if `WarmupManager.isWarmingUp(uuid)` then call `WarmupManager.cancelWarmup(uuid, \"You moved!\")`.\n- `ServerEntityCombatEvents.AFTER_DAMAGE` (or `ServerPlayerEvents.AFTER_DAMAGE`) to cancel warm‑up on damage with the same method, reason \"You took damage!\".\nMake sure the event callbacks run on the server thread.\nProvide the exact registration code and any required imports.\n",
      "depends_on": ["s4"]
    }
  ]
}

{
  "title": "LoRA vs Full Fine‑Tuning on M3 Ultra",
  "kind": "research",
  "summary": "Compare Low‑Rank Adaptation (LoRA) and full fine‑tuning for a ~4 B parameter model using Apple's MLX on an M3 Ultra. Produce memory requirements, rough training time for 5 000 examples, quality trade‑offs, and a concise one‑page recommendation on which approach to start with.",
